        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A garage floor that has hosted a leaky car for five years holds oil a quarter inch or more into the slab. Degreaser and a brush clean the top layer, the floor looks better for a day, and then the stain wicks back up as more oil migrates to the surface. Getting it out means working with how concrete actually behaves: draw the oil out with a poultice, digest it with microbes, or loosen it with heat, and give each method enough time to work. This guide covers all three for garage floors, basement slabs, shop floors and old driveway stains.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="why-it-stays" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Old Oil Will Not Scrub Out of a Slab
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete looks solid, but it is full of capillary pores, the tiny channels left behind as water evaporates from the mix during curing. Oil travels through those channels by capillary action, the same way coffee climbs a paper towel. It moves down and sideways, and the darker halo you see around an old stain is oil that has spread laterally below the surface. A garage floor with a smooth troweled finish slows this down; an old, worn slab with the top cream layer gone lets it travel faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why surface scrubbing disappoints on old stains. A degreaser emulsifies the oil it touches, and you rinse that away, but most of the oil sits deeper than the cleaner reaches during a 10-minute dwell. When the slab dries, oil from below wicks upward toward the drier surface and the stain reappears, sometimes nearly as dark as before. People often describe this as the stain coming back. Really, it never left.
          </p>

          <p>
            The methods that work on old stains all address depth. A poultice pulls oil upward into an absorbent layer over many hours. Enzyme and microbial cleaners break hydrocarbons down into carbon dioxide and water in place, without needing to bring them to the surface. Hot water and steam thin heavy oil so it flows out more readily when combined with a cleaner. Each takes longer than a degreaser rinse, and each works better when you give it more time rather than more scrubbing.
          </p>

          <p>
            One honest expectation to set: some very old stains never disappear completely. Oil that has been in a slab for a decade can leave a faint shadow even after a thorough job. The realistic goal for those is a stain that blends with the surrounding floor and no longer feels slick or transfers to shoes. For fresh spills on a driveway, which is a different and faster job, see our guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from asphalt and concrete driveways</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="assess" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Assess the Stain Before You Pick a Method
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by cleaning the surface so you can see what you are working with. Sweep, then scrub the stained area with a strong alkaline degreaser and rinse. This removes the surface film and dirt, and it tells you how much of the stain is on top versus inside the slab. Let the concrete dry for a full day before you judge. If the stain is mostly gone, you are done. If a dark area remains, it is a subsurface stain and needs one of the deeper methods.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, check whether the concrete is sealed. Drip water on a clean area near the stain. If it beads, there is a sealer or coating; if it darkens the concrete within a minute, the slab is bare. Poultices and enzymes need bare, porous concrete to work, because they rely on oil moving through the pores. On a sealed floor, the stain may be trapped in or under the sealer, and you will need to strip that section before deep treatment makes any difference.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stain profile</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely depth</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best first method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical timeline</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dark, still slightly slick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shallow to moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Degreaser, then poultice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dull, dark, months old</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poultice, repeated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-7 days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Faded halo, years old</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Enzyme cleaner, kept damp</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 weeks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under a sealer or coating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">In or below film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip the area first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gummy residue on top</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface plus depth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Scrape, hot water, then poultice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-5 days</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Finally, think about what you plan to do with the floor afterward. If you intend to coat a garage floor with epoxy, the standard is much higher: coatings fail over oil contamination, often lifting in that exact spot within a year. Coating manufacturers commonly specify a water-drop test and a clean, degreased surface before etching or grinding. If you are only after appearance, a stain that has faded to a light shadow may be good enough, and a penetrating sealer will keep it from getting worse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="poultice" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Poultice Method for Set-In Oil
          </h2>

          <p>
            A poultice is a paste made of an absorbent powder mixed with a liquid that dissolves or emulsifies oil. You spread it over the stain, cover it so it dries slowly, and as the liquid evaporates upward it carries dissolved oil out of the pores and deposits it into the powder. Stone restorers have used this approach on marble and granite for decades, and it works on concrete for the same reasons.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Making a poultice</h3>

          <p>
            The powder can be diatomaceous earth, plain clay kitty litter ground to a fine powder, powdered whiting, or even all-purpose flour in a pinch. Diatomaceous earth is the most effective common option because it is extremely porous. The liquid is where you choose your chemistry: a concentrated water-based degreaser for most stains, or acetone or mineral spirits for very heavy oil on bare concrete in a well-ventilated space. Mix to the consistency of peanut butter, thick enough to stay put without running.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some products come premixed. Pour-N-Restore is a liquid you pour and brush in that dries into a powder holding the oil, which is a poultice in a bottle. Several stone-care brands sell poultice powders that you mix with water or a solvent. They cost more than homemade, roughly $15 to $30 for a bottle that treats a few stains at the time of writing, but they take the guesswork out of consistency.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Applying it</h3>

          <p>
            Clean and dry the stain first. Lightly dampen the concrete with the same liquid you used in the poultice so the pores are primed to move oil. Spread the paste about a quarter inch thick, extending an inch beyond the visible edge of the stain, since oil has traveled farther than you can see. Cover it with plastic sheeting and tape the edges, then poke a few small holes in the plastic. Slow drying gives the poultice more time to pull oil.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave it for 24 to 48 hours, then remove the plastic and let the poultice dry completely. Scrape it off with a plastic putty knife, sweep up the residue and dispose of it as oily waste. Rinse the area, let it dry for a day, and look. A moderate stain often fades by half or more on the first pass. Repeat as many times as the stain keeps improving. Three to five rounds is not unusual on a garage floor stain that has built up over years.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SOLVENT SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Acetone and mineral spirits are flammable.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you mix a solvent poultice, work with the garage door open, keep water heaters, furnaces and pilot lights in mind, and never use solvents near an ignition source. Wear chemical-resistant gloves and eye protection. A water-based degreaser poultice is slower but avoids the fire risk entirely.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="enzymes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Enzyme and Microbial Cleaners
          </h2>

          <p>
            Enzyme and microbial oil removers take a different approach. Instead of pulling oil out, they deliver hydrocarbon-eating microbes and the enzymes those microbes produce. Products in this category include Terminator-HSD, which is sold as a powder you mix with water, and several liquid concentrates aimed at garage and shop floors. The microbes break oil down into carbon dioxide and water over days or weeks, which means they keep working in the pores after the visible surface is clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            The method is simple but needs patience. Clean the surface first so the microbes are not fighting dirt. Apply the mixture according to the label, work it in with a stiff brush, and keep the area damp. Microbes need moisture to stay active, so mist the area or cover it with plastic, especially indoors where the air is dry. Avoid rinsing for at least the time the label specifies, often a day or longer. Many owners commonly report that a stain looks unchanged for several days and then fades noticeably over the following week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature affects results. Microbial cleaners slow down sharply in cold weather, and most labels suggest working when the concrete is above roughly 50 degrees Fahrenheit. In an unheated garage in winter, a poultice is the better choice. Also avoid using bleach, acid or a strong disinfectant on the area before or during treatment, because they kill the microbes. Rinse away any prior degreaser thoroughly before applying an enzyme product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Enzymes shine on large, faded stains where a poultice would require a lot of material, and on surfaces where you want to avoid solvents. They are also safer around landscaping and pets than most alternatives. The trade-off is speed: if you need a floor ready to coat by Saturday, start with a poultice. For ranked options in this category, see our roundup of the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Heat, Hot Water and Pressure Washing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil gets thinner as it warms, and thinner oil moves more readily out of concrete pores. That is why commercial floor cleaning crews use hot water pressure washers on fuel station aprons and shop floors. A hot water unit combined with a degreaser removes far more oil per pass than cold water. Most homeowner pressure washers are cold water only, but you can still bring heat into the process.
          </p>

          <p>
            One approach is to pour hot water from a kettle onto the stain, apply degreaser while the concrete is warm, scrub, and rinse. Another is to work on a sunny afternoon when the slab has warmed up, keeping the cleaner from drying out by misting it. Some people use a heat gun at a moderate setting to warm a stain before applying a poultice; keep it moving and do not overheat one spot, because rapid heating can cause surface spalling on concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold water pressure washing alone rarely removes old oil, but it is a good rinse after a degreaser or poultice because it flushes emulsified oil out of the surface texture. Use a 15-degree or 25-degree tip and hold it 8 to 12 inches from the surface. A rotating surface cleaner attachment gives a more even finish on a large area and avoids the striping that a hand wand leaves. Our guide to the <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">best surface cleaners for pressure washers</a> covers which attachments match which machines.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have a garage floor with years of buildup, renting a hot water pressure washer from an equipment rental shop for a day can be the most efficient path, at roughly $100 to $200 at the time of writing. Combined with a degreaser and a good wet vacuum to collect the wash water, it handles the whole floor at once instead of chasing individual stains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="garage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. A Full Workflow for a Stained Garage Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Garage floors usually have several stains of different ages, plus general grime. Working in order saves time. Start by moving everything off the floor and sweeping thoroughly. Scrape any thick, gummy deposits with a floor scraper or putty knife. Then apply a heavy alkaline degreaser to the whole floor with a pump sprayer, scrub with a deck brush or floor machine, and rinse with a wet vacuum or squeegee toward the door.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the floor dry for 24 to 48 hours and map what remains. Mark the stains with tape or chalk. Fresh and medium stains get a poultice; large, faded patches get an enzyme treatment. You can run both at once on different stains. Cover the poultices, keep the enzyme areas damp, and leave the floor alone for a couple of days. Then remove, rinse, dry and reassess.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Garage Floor Timeline at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Day 1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sweep, scrape, whole-floor degrease</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Day 3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Map stains, apply poultice or enzyme</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Day 5-7</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Remove, rinse, reassess, repeat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Week 2+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Seal or coat once fully dry</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Repeat the targeted treatments until improvement stops. On most garage floors, two rounds handle the majority of stains and a third clears the rest down to faint shadows. Then decide on protection. A penetrating sealer keeps the bare concrete look and resists future stains; an epoxy or polyaspartic coating gives a fully sealed, easy-wipe surface. If you are going the coating route, follow the manufacturer's prep, which usually means grinding or etching after degreasing, and see our picks for the <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">best garage floor epoxy</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collect and dispose of wash water responsibly. Garage floor runoff from degreasing contains oil and should not be washed down the driveway into the storm drain. A wet vacuum makes collection easy. Let the water sit so oil separates, and take the oily portion to your local household hazardous waste drop-off. Used poultice material and absorbent can generally go in household trash in small quantities, but check local rules.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="finish" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Finishing, Testing and Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before you seal or coat, confirm the floor is genuinely clean. Do a simple water test: sprinkle water across the cleaned area and the surrounding concrete. Clean concrete absorbs water evenly and darkens. If water beads or sits on top in the stained area, oil is still present near the surface, and a sealer or coating will not bond there. Treat it again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people try to cover a remaining shadow with concrete paint or a solid stain. That can work cosmetically on a slab that is otherwise clean, but paint over oil is a common cause of peeling, so the same cleanliness test applies. If you want a uniform look without coating, cleaning the entire floor with a <a href="/best-driveway-cleaner/">concrete and driveway cleaner</a> evens out the color so treated spots do not stand out as lighter patches.
          </p>

          <p>
            For bare concrete you want to keep looking natural, a penetrating silane or siloxane sealer reduces how fast oil soaks in. It does not make the floor stainproof, but it buys you time, often enough to blot a drip before it sets. Our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">best concrete sealers</a> breaks down which types suit garage floors versus exterior slabs. Reapply on the schedule the manufacturer specifies, usually every few years for penetrating products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Last, fix the source. A recurring leak will undo any amount of cleaning. Park over a drip pan or containment mat until the car is repaired, and keep a bag of absorbent and a bottle of degreaser in the garage. Treating a drip within an hour costs a few minutes; treating it after a year costs a week of poultices.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting one scrub to fix a years-old stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old oil sits well below the surface, so one round of degreaser only cleans the top. People give up after a single attempt because the stain looks the same once dry. Plan on several rounds of a poultice or a week or more of enzyme treatment, and judge progress by comparing dry results between rounds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the poultice dry too fast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A poultice works while its liquid is evaporating and carrying oil upward. Left uncovered in sun or wind, it dries in an hour and pulls very little. Cover it with plastic, poke a few small holes, and let it work for a day or two before removing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Killing the microbes before they start</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Enzyme and microbial cleaners are living products. Using bleach, acid or a disinfecting cleaner just before applying them, or letting the treated area dry out, stops them working. Rinse off previous chemicals thoroughly, keep the area damp and stay within the temperature range on the label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating over a stain that looks clean</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy and paint fail over oil even when the stain has faded to almost nothing. If water beads on the spot instead of soaking in, oil is still near the surface. Keep treating until water absorbs evenly, then follow the coating maker's etch or grind instructions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using solvents in a closed garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acetone, mineral spirits and lacquer thinner produce flammable vapor that can reach a water heater or furnace pilot. Always open the garage door, ventilate, and keep ignition sources off or far away. If ventilation is poor, use a water-based degreaser poultice or an enzyme product instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you get oil completely out of concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fresh stains, usually yes. Stains a few months old often come out fully with repeated poultices or enzyme treatment. Stains that have soaked into a slab for many years may leave a faint shadow even after a thorough job, because some oil sits too deep to reach. The aim on those is a stain that no longer feels slick and blends with the floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best powder for a concrete oil poultice?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Diatomaceous earth is the most effective common choice because it is extremely porous and holds a lot of oil. Finely ground clay kitty litter and powdered whiting also work well and cost less. Flour and cornstarch work in a pinch. Mix the powder with a degreaser or solvent into a thick paste that stays in place without running.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should an enzyme cleaner stay on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the label, but most need at least a day without rinsing and keep working for a week or more as long as the area stays damp. Microbes slow down in cold weather, so results come faster when the concrete is above roughly 50 degrees Fahrenheit. Avoid using bleach or acid nearby while they work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Coca-Cola or WD-40 remove oil from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is a good choice for set-in stains. Cola is mildly acidic and can lift a little surface grime, but it does not dissolve oil and leaves a sticky residue. WD-40 can loosen fresh grease but adds more petroleum to the slab. A degreaser poultice or enzyme cleaner does far more for old stains.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a garage floor coating cover oil stains?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It will hide them for a while, but coatings bond poorly to oil-contaminated concrete and commonly lift or bubble in those spots. Coating manufacturers specify a clean, degreased surface before etching or grinding. Clean stains until water soaks in evenly, then prepare the floor according to the coating instructions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a hot water pressure washer worth renting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a whole garage floor or a heavily stained shop slab, often yes. Hot water thins oil so it releases from the pores much more readily than with cold water. Rental shops typically charge roughly $100 to $200 a day at the time of writing. For one or two spots, a poultice is cheaper and just as effective.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop oil from staining the floor again?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fix the leak, use a drip pan or containment mat in the meantime, and seal the concrete. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer slows oil absorption enough to blot spills before they set; an epoxy or polyaspartic coating makes the floor wipe clean. Keep absorbent and degreaser handy so drips get treated within the hour.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

